## Working with Uninitialized Memory

`MaybeUninit[T]` lets you create values without immediately initializing them. Useful for performance optimizations and custom data structures.

### Why MaybeUninit?

Normally, XIOM requires every variable to be initialized:

```xiom
// Normal: must initialize
let x: Int = 42;

// With MaybeUninit: can defer initialization
let uninit = MaybeUninit[Int].uninit();  // uninitialized Int
```

### Filling Later

```xiom
let mut slot = MaybeUninit[Vec[Int]]().uninit();

// Later: initialize
unsafe {
  slot.write(Vec[Int]());
}

// Even later: take the initialized value
unsafe {
  let v = slot.assume_init();
  v.push(42);
}
```

### Common Pattern: Array Initialization

```xiom
// Initialize an array of Vecs without default-constructing each
let mut slots = MaybeUninit[[10]Vec[Int]]().uninit();

// Fill slots one at a time
unsafe {
  slots[0].write(Vec[Int]());
  slots[0].assume_init().push(1);
}
```

### Safety Rules

1. `.write()`: only call once per slot (double write is UB)
2. `.assume_init()`: only after `.write()` has been called
3. Drop manually if initialized but not consumed

### When to Use

- Custom Vec implementations
- Fixed-capacity containers
- Performance-critical initialization paths
- Avoiding default construction overhead

### Typical Workflow

```xiom
let mut value = MaybeUninit[Int].uninit();
unsafe { value.write(42); }
let initialized = unsafe { value.assume_init() };
// initialized is now a normal Int = 42
```

